From 61036a7c75b8b674c7e3f7e1744feb1fb6fade91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: l4h <l4h@localhost>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 23:22:24 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] x86: what KVM needs from the VCPU interface

A guest kernel that implements KVM's API on seL4 VCPUs needs more of them
than a VMM written for seL4 does, and any guest can now hold a VCPU:

- Userspace can turn on only the controls listed here.  The others use
  VMCS address fields that nothing sets, so a guest could point a
  virtual APIC, PML log or posted-interrupt descriptor at host physical
  page 0.  MOV-DR, RDPMC, MWAIT and MONITOR always exit: the
  debug registers and counters are not switched.  So do #AC, #DB and
  #MC, whatever the exception bitmap says: a guest can make delivering
  #AC or #DB raise it again, a loop that no host interrupt ends
  (CVE-2015-5307, CVE-2015-8104), and a machine check is the host's.
  With the hardware debug API, DR0-3 are cleared before a VM entry, as
  a guest DR7 could otherwise enable another thread's breakpoints.
- VMEnter returns the same MRs for a fault and for a notification, with
  CR2 and the exit and IDT-vectoring information added, so one call
  gives what KVM otherwise reads with more.  When the VCPU has not run,
  the exit fields read 0 and INTERRUPT_INFO_MR keeps the event.
- The guest's CR2 is kept across exits, which other threads' page faults
  would overwrite, in the fault MRs and through WriteMSR.
- WriteVMCS takes the TSC offset and the entry instruction length;
  ReadVMCS the TSC offset.  ReadMSR and WriteMSR take KERNEL_GS_BASE.
  WriteMSR refuses noncanonical KERNEL_GS_BASE, LSTAR and CSTAR
  (seL4_InvalidArgument): KERNEL_GS_BASE is loaded with WRMSR in kernel
  mode before the VM entry, where such a value faulted in the kernel.
  Canonical is in 57 bits on a CPU with 5-level paging, as the CPU
  checks it and as a guest with CR4.LA57 writes it.
- invept reaches every core that has entered a VM.  A local invept
  leaves the translations of a frame unmapped while its VCPU runs on
  another core, or on the core a VCPU has left.  FlushTLB is for what
  the guest's own paging produced: it flushes the VCPU's VPID before the
  VCPU next runs, which needs no other core, as one the VCPU arrives on
  flushes it then.  An invept there interrupted every core that had run
  a VM each time a guest flushed its TLB, while holding the kernel lock.
- Each VCPU has its own XCR0 (SEL4_VCPU_MSR_XCR0), validated as XSETBV
  would (x87 on, AVX with SSE, AVX-512, MPX and AMX state only whole),
  loaded just before VM entry and replaced by the kernel's at the exit,
  and only when it differs from the kernel's.
- A VCPU that arrives on a core it did not last run on has its VPID
  invalidated there: the guest may have flushed its TLB elsewhere since.
  So does a VCPU given a VPID, which its last holder may have used there.
  Those flushes come after the kernel lock is released, when another
  core may have taken the VCPU's VPID (findFreeVPID()): they then flush
  the one the VMCS still holds, which the entry runs on.
- A thread about to enter guest mode makes its VCPU its core's current
  one before the kernel lock is released, not after.  An invocation on
  another core then always finds it there and takes it out of guest mode
  first, rather than loading a VMCS that core is about to enter with.
  The VCPU takes its VPID there too: every core shares the VPID table,
  and two cores could give the same VPID to two VCPUs, of which the
  table then names one, so revoking an I/O port cap left the ports to
  the other.  WriteRegisters also takes the VCPU out of guest mode
  first, as its exit would store the guest's registers over the new.
- A thread that loses its VCPU in guest mode (SetTCB, the VCPU's
  deletion), preempted there or on another core, would enter guest mode
  again with none: its VMEnter ends then, as a notification ends it.
  An exit that, once it has the lock, finds its VMEnter ended so is
  dropped, and the guest repeats what exited; one whose VMCS another
  core took loads it again.  A deleted thread is unbound from its VCPU,
  which kept pointing at the freed TCB.
- An exit reads its interrupt information and qualification, and a
  failed VMLAUNCH or VMRESUME restores the kernel's XCR0, before taking
  the kernel lock: an invocation on another core can take the VMCS while
  this core waits, after which VMREAD read garbage (the IRQ handled, the
  qualification reported) and the failed entry found no current VCPU.
- A notification for a thread in RunningVM on another core interrupts
  that core only when the thread is running there.  Otherwise the
  signalling core ends the VMEnter itself: the IPI handler would take
  the VCPU back with a remote call of its own, inside the signaller's,
  which overwrote its arguments and barrier and could leave a VMCS
  active on two cores.
- An exit userspace does not see (an external interrupt, or one a stall
  IPI drops) may have come while the CPU delivered an event.  That
  event is injected again when the guest resumes, from the IDT-vectoring
  information, unless userspace has injected one since; the guest
  otherwise lost an interrupt or exception its VMM injected.  An exit
  for a failed VM entry leaves the launch state and the entry event as
  they were, as the CPU does.
- A failed VMLAUNCH or VMRESUME of a 64-bit-guest kernel left the
  thread's GS base in GS_BASE and the stack pointer in the VCPU's
  registers: the kernel then took its per-core state from an address
  userspace chose and ran its C code on the VCPU object.  It now swaps
  GS back and moves to the kernel stack, as an exit does.
- A thread re-entering guest mode without the kernel lock (after a
  remote-call IPI, or a stale MCS timer interrupt, while the lock was
  free) made its VCPU current then, so it could load a VMCS another
  core had just taken.  It takes the lock first, and checks its bound
  notification there too: one signalled from another core went
  unchecked if the thread was switched out and back in since the IPI.
- EnableIOPort took a range with its low port above its high one, which
  enabled ports outside the I/O port cap, and on a VCPU without a VPID
  the ports outlived the cap, as revoking it finds the VCPU by VPID: the
  VCPU now takes one.  DisableIOPort refuses such a range too.
- WriteVMCS takes only the active and HLT activity states: in shutdown
  or wait-for-SIPI no host interrupt ends guest mode.
- WBINVD always exits where the CPU can make it, as INVD does: a
  guest's would flush the caches the core shares with seL4 and other
  guests, holding off the core's interrupts meanwhile.
- The guest runs on the TCB's FPU state, without two swaps per exit:
  userspace needs the guest's registers, which seL4 kept in the VCPU
  where nothing could read them.

When seL4 itself runs under a hypervisor without VMCS shadowing, each
VMREAD and VMWRITE is an exit to it, and so is each WRMSR of a syscall
MSR.  So VM exits and entries do only what they need:

- The syscall MSRs (STAR, LSTAR, CSTAR, FMASK) switch through the VMCS
  MSR-load lists instead of eight WRMSRs per exit.
- An exit reads only the fields its reason defines; the controls and the
  entry event come from what was last written, and VMEnter writes RIP,
  the controls and the entry event only when they change.  An exit
  forgets the RIP, which userspace learns again from the exit's MRs: a
  thread resumed after a suspend in guest mode enters with the RIP it
  brings, not where the guest stopped.  RSP and CR3
  are for ReadVMCS (CR3_MR is not filled in), and CR0 is read back only
  when the guest owns some of it.
- Unmapping a page from an EPT invalidates every core that may hold its
  translation; it invalidated none, so a guest kept reaching a frame
  after it was unmapped (seen once mapping stopped invalidating).
  Mapping does no invept: it only fills not-present entries, which no
  TLB caches.  So deleting a root invalidates it on those cores: what
  was below it can no longer be found to unmap, and a root later made
  at the same address would otherwise run on its translations.
  INVEPT gets the whole EPTP the VMCS has, not just the root's
  address: the CPU ignores the other bits, but nested KVM compares them
  and, with the address alone, invalidated nothing.  A core records
  that it enters VMs before it releases the kernel lock to do so, as it
  looks up the EPT root after: one deleted in between was freed with no
  invept there.  Deleting an ASID pool invalidates the EPT roots in it,
  as deleting each root does: unmapping below them no longer finds them.
- VT-x needs an x86_64 kernel: KernelVTX now depends on it.  VMEnter
  reads its three call MRs from registers, which a 32-bit kernel does
  not have enough of for a thread without an IPC buffer.
